<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Keeps one queue worker alive across terminal sessions.

    Written because the run keeps dying with the session that started it: a
    worker launched with `nohup ... &` from a shell is still a child of that
    shell's process group, and table detection lost roughly two days that way
    twice. A run measured in days cannot be owned by a terminal window.

    ONE worker. Two against SQLite contend, and the failures look like
    unrelated bugs — see the runbook.

    Queue order matters and is deliberate.

    `ingestion` first, because reading a document is cheap and finding its
    tables is not. The 2,490 fetched judgments are almost entirely native text
    — 15 of 16 pages in the first sampled document, 9 of 9 in the next — so
    extraction is pdftotext rather than OCR: hours for all of them. Table
    detection runs about two minutes a page with thousands of pages left.
    Behind the tables the judgments would wait days; ahead of them, an
    afternoon.

    Then the table tranches in their own order, chosen so that pages holding a
    value that could not be located are examined first, since cells are what
    let such a value be found.

    `tables-later` is deliberately absent, and this is the change that makes
    table detection finishable at all. 41,570 eligible pages have no detection
    and the queue holds a fraction of them, so draining everything was never on
    the table: at the rate this machine manages — about five to eight pages an
    hour once the 2.2 GB detector starts paging an 8 GB machine — the full set
    is roughly a year, and even a healthy fifteen an hour is 115 days.

    The two tranches kept are 1,551 jobs against pages holding values the
    locator could not find, which is where a cell actually buys something: the
    value becomes locatable, and a locatable value can be scored and certified.
    `tables-later` is bulk fill over pages that already read well, and its 1,746
    jobs stay queued and unworked rather than being deleted, so the decision can
    be reversed by putting the tranche back in this list.

    `court` is absent: judgment fetching is paced by its own agent, not by this
    queue.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.civiclens.queue-worker</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin/php</string>
        <!--
            Unbuffered, because this log is the only account of a run measured
            in days.

            launchd redirects stdout to a file, and libc block-buffers a
            non-tty in 4 KB chunks, so entries appeared roughly six hours late.
            Twice in one night that turned a diagnosis into a guess: a page that
            had run 3h 14m was invisible while it ran, and the fix for it could
            not be confirmed afterwards because the log had not caught up.

            A queue worker writes a line or two per job, so line-buffering costs
            nothing and buys an account that is true when read.
        -->
        <string>-d</string>
        <string>output_buffering=0</string>
        <string>-d</string>
        <string>implicit_flush=1</string>
        <string>artisan</string>
        <string>queue:work</string>
        <string>--queue=ingestion,tables,tables-candidates</string>
        <!--
            Recycled hourly rather than run for ever. A long-lived PHP worker
            accumulates memory, and this pipeline has already met three
            exhaustion bugs at 128 MB; KeepAlive restarts it immediately.
        -->
        <string>--max-time=3600</string>
        <!--
            The job's own $tries wins over this, which is why a timed-out table
            job leaves no row in failed_jobs. Recorded in the runbook.
        -->
        <string>--tries=1</string>
        <!--
            At least the longest job's own timeout, and it was not.

            ExtractAcquiredArtifact declares timeout = 1500 and says in as many
            words that the worker must be given at least as much or it stops the
            job before that applies. This said 900. A 587-page document takes
            longer than that to read, so the worker killed it at fifteen
            minutes, the reservation lapsed, and the job came round again — the
            same document read three times over, 1,761 stored pages for 587
            real ones.

            The ordering that has to hold, and now does:

                job timeout (1500) <= worker --timeout (1800) < retry_after (2100)

            The last inequality matters as much as the first: a reservation
            reclaimed while its job is still running dispatches that job a
            second time, which is the duplication above by another route.
            QueueTimeoutOrderingTest pins all of it.
        -->
        <string>--timeout=1800</string>
    </array>


    <!--
        launchd does not give a login shell's PATH. It hands over roughly
        /usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin, and every external tool this pipeline
        needs — pdftoppm, pdftotext, tesseract, soffice — lives in Homebrew's
        prefix. Without this the first run of this agent consumed 3,445 table
        jobs and failed every one with "Page rasterization could not read the
        document", which is what a missing pdftoppm looks like from inside PHP:
        not a crash, a page that cannot be read. The queue emptied, the failed
        table grew, and the examined count barely moved.

        Every optional tool in this pipeline fails closed and therefore fails
        quietly. An agent that cannot see them does not announce it.
    -->
    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/opt/homebrew/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin</string>
    </dict>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/muttakinrahman/Documents/New OpenCode Project/CivicLense/.worktrees/v2-ingestion</string>

    <!-- Restart when it exits, including after --max-time. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>

    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
        A crash loop should not become a fork bomb. launchd throttles respawns
        to this many seconds apart.
    -->
    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>30</integer>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/Users/muttakinrahman/Documents/New OpenCode Project/CivicLense/.worktrees/v2-ingestion/storage/logs/launchd-queue-worker.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/Users/muttakinrahman/Documents/New OpenCode Project/CivicLense/.worktrees/v2-ingestion/storage/logs/launchd-queue-worker.log</string>

    <key>ProcessType</key>
    <string>Background</string>

    <!--
        Nice it. Table detection is CPU-bound and measurably doubles in wall
        time when it competes; this keeps it out of the way of interactive work
        rather than fighting it.
    -->
    <key>LowPriorityIO</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
